{
  "$schema": "https://schemastore.azurewebsites.net/schemas/json/sarif-2.1.0-rtm.6.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "DevOpsShield-SecurityScanner",
          "organization": "CAD4DevOps Inc. (DevOps Shield)",
          "product": "DevOps Shield",
          "shortDescription": {
            "text": "DevOps Shield - An innovative cybersecurity platform for DevOps.",
            "markdown": "DevOps Shield - An innovative cybersecurity platform for DevOps."
          },
          "fullDescription": {
            "text": "DevOps Shield - Your DevOps. We Protect It. DevOps Shield is the ultimate cybersecurity platform designed to secure your Azure DevOps, GitHub, and GitLab environments. *DevSecOps Security Scanner. *Continuous DevOps Security Monitoring. *Policy Management. *Expert Advisory."
          },
          "fullName": "DevOps Shield Security Scanner",
          "version": "1.0.0.0",
          "semanticVersion": "1.0.0.0",
          "downloadUri": "https://hub.docker.com/r/devopsshield/devopsshield",
          "informationUri": "https://www.devopsshield.com/",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "DOS4010",
              "name": "GhOrganizationPoliciesNopermissionRepositoryBasePermissions",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Base permissions for an organization should be set to 'No permission'. Members will only be able to clone and pull public and internal repositories. To give a member additional access, you'll need to add them to an authorized team or make them a collaborator on individual repositories. Base permissions to the organization's repositories apply to all members and excludes outside collaborators. Since organization members can have permissions from multiple sources, members and collaborators who have been granted a higher level of access than the base permissions will retain their higher permission privileges. By default, members of an organization will have Read permissions to the organization's repositories. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/organizations/managing-user-access-to-your-organizations-repositories/managing-repository-roles/setting-base-permissions-for-an-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Access' section of the sidebar, click Member privileges. \n3. Under the 'Base permissions' section. \n4. Ensure the 'No permission' setting is selected.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Access' section of the sidebar, click Member privileges. \n3. Under the 'Base permissions' section. \n4. Ensure the 'No permission' setting is selected.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4010](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4010)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4010 - Organization - Ensure Repository Base permissions is set to 'No permission' (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4010"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4015",
              "name": "GhOrganizationPoliciesDisableRepositoryForking",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Repository forking should be disabled. Organizations should never allow private or internal repositories to be forked. If enabled, forking is allowed on private, internal, and public repositories. If disabled, forking is only allowed on public repositories. This setting is also configurable per-repository. By default, new organizations are configured to disallow the forking of private and internal repositories. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/code-security/getting-started/best-practices-for-preventing-data-leaks-in-your-organization#prevent-data-leaks https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/organizations/managing-organization-settings/managing-the-forking-policy-for-your-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Access' section of the sidebar, click Member privileges. \n3. Under the 'Repository forking' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Allow forking of private and internal repositories' setting is disabled.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Access' section of the sidebar, click Member privileges. \n3. Under the 'Repository forking' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Allow forking of private and internal repositories' setting is disabled.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4015](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4015)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4015 - Organization - Disable Repository Forking (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4015"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4025",
              "name": "GhOrganizationPoliciesRestrictRepositoryCreationPublic",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Restrict repository creation to private or internal. Restricting repository creation in your organization to help prevent sensitive information from being exposed. To protect your organization's data, you can configure permissions for creating repositories in your organization. Members should be able to create only private or internal repositories, visible to organization members with permission. Members will not be able to create public repositories, visible to anyone. Outside collaborators can never create repositories. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/code-security/getting-started/best-practices-for-preventing-data-leaks-in-your-organization#prevent-data-leaks https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/organizations/managing-organization-settings/restricting-repository-creation-in-your-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Access' section of the sidebar, click Member privileges. \n3. Under the 'Repository creation' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Public' setting is disabled.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Access' section of the sidebar, click Member privileges. \n3. Under the 'Repository creation' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Public' setting is disabled.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4025](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4025)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4025 - Organization - Disable Public Repository Creation - Disallow members to create public repositories (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4025"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4030",
              "name": "GhOrganizationPoliciesRestrictRepositoryCreationToInternal",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Restricting repository creation in your organization to help prevent sensitive information from being exposed. To protect your organization's data, you can configure permissions for creating repositories in your organization. Members should be able to create internal repositories, visible to all enterprise members. Outside collaborators can never create repositories. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/code-security/getting-started/best-practices-for-preventing-data-leaks-in-your-organization#prevent-data-leaks https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/organizations/managing-organization-settings/restricting-repository-creation-in-your-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Access' section of the sidebar, click Member privileges. \n3. Under the 'Repository creation' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Internal' setting is enabled.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Access' section of the sidebar, click Member privileges. \n3. Under the 'Repository creation' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Internal' setting is enabled.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4030](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4030)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4030 - Organization - Restrict Repository Creation to Internal - Allow members to create internal repositories (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4030"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4035",
              "name": "GhOrganizationPoliciesRestrictRepositoryCreationToPrivate",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Restricting repository creation in your organization to help prevent sensitive information from being exposed. To protect your organization's data, you can configure permissions for creating repositories in your organization. Members should be able to create private repositories, visible to organization members with permission. Outside collaborators can never create repositories. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/code-security/getting-started/best-practices-for-preventing-data-leaks-in-your-organization#prevent-data-leaks https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/organizations/managing-organization-settings/restricting-repository-creation-in-your-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Access' section of the sidebar, click Member privileges. \n3. Under the 'Repository creation' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Private' setting is enabled.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Access' section of the sidebar, click Member privileges. \n3. Under the 'Repository creation' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Private' setting is enabled.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4035](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4035)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4035 - Organization - Restrict Repository Creation to Private - Allow members to create private repositories (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4035"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4210",
              "name": "GhOrganizationAuthenticationSecurityRequire2Fa",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Requiring an additional authentication method adds another level of security for your organization. Require two-factor authentication for everyone in your organization. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/organizations/keeping-your-organization-secure/managing-two-factor-authentication-for-your-organization/requiring-two-factor-authentication-in-your-organization https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/securing-your-account-with-two-factor-authentication-2fa/configuring-two-factor-authentication"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Security' section of the sidebar, click on Authentication security. \n3. Under the 'Two-factor authentication' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Require two-factor authentication for everyone in the organization' setting is enabled.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Security' section of the sidebar, click on Authentication security. \n3. Under the 'Two-factor authentication' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Require two-factor authentication for everyone in the organization' setting is enabled.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4210](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4210)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4210 - Organization - Require two-factor authentication in your organization (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4210"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4240",
              "name": "GhOrganizationMemberShouldHave2FaEnabled",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every organization member should have two-factor authentication enabled. The TwoFactorDisabled property is true ONLY when GitHub explicitly reports the member appears in the ?filter=2fa_disabled audit; null or false means 2FA is enabled. Members with 2FA disabled are a common attack vector for org takeover and should be required to enroll before accessing sensitive resources. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/organizations/keeping-your-organization-secure/managing-two-factor-authentication-for-your-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings -> Authentication security. \n2. Enable 'Require two-factor authentication for everyone in this organization'. \n3. Reach out to non-compliant members; they will be removed from the org if they do not enroll within the GitHub-mandated grace period.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings -> Authentication security. \n2. Enable 'Require two-factor authentication for everyone in this organization'. \n3. Reach out to non-compliant members; they will be removed from the org if they do not enroll within the GitHub-mandated grace period.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4240](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4240)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4240 - Organization - Organization Member - Should have two-factor authentication enabled (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4240"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4260",
              "name": "GhOrganizationOutsidecollaboratorShouldNotHaveAdmin",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Outside (external) collaborators should never have admin permission across the organization. Admin grants the ability to add/remove collaborators, modify protected branches, and access secrets - powers that should be reserved for trusted org members. External collaborators with admin access are a frequent supply-chain attack vector and a top finding in GitHub security audits. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/organizations/managing-user-access-to-your-organizations-repositories/managing-outside-collaborators/adding-outside-collaborators-to-repositories-in-your-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization -> Outside collaborators. \n2. For each collaborator, review their permissions. \n3. Downgrade any 'Admin' permission to 'Maintain', 'Write', or lower as appropriate.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization -> Outside collaborators. \n2. For each collaborator, review their permissions. \n3. Downgrade any 'Admin' permission to 'Maintain', 'Write', or lower as appropriate.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4260](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4260)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4260 - Organization - Outside Collaborator - Should not have admin permission at the organization level (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4260"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4300",
              "name": "GhOrganizationActionsPoliciesRestrictDefaultWorkflowPermissionsToRead",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "As a good security practice, you should grant the GITHUB_TOKEN the least required access. Read should be the default permissions granted to the GITHUB_TOKEN when running workflows in your organization. You can specify more granular permissions in the workflow using YAML. Workflows have read permissions in the repository for the contents and packages scopes only. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/security-guides/automatic-token-authentication#modifying-the-permissions-for-the-github_token https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/actions/security-guides/security-hardening-for-github-actions#restricting-permissions-for-tokens https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/organizations/managing-organization-settings/disabling-or-limiting-github-actions-for-your-organization#setting-the-permissions-of-the-github_token-for-your-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Code, planning, and automation' section of the sidebar, click on Actions -> General. \n3. Under the 'Workflow permissions' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Read repository contents and packages permissions' setting is selected.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Code, planning, and automation' section of the sidebar, click on Actions -> General. \n3. Under the 'Workflow permissions' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Read repository contents and packages permissions' setting is selected.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4300](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4300)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4300 - Organization - Workflow permissions - Default workflow permissions granted to the GITHUB_TOKEN should be restricted to read-only access (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4300"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4320",
              "name": "GhOrganizationActionsPoliciesRestrictManagingPullRequests",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Prevent GitHub Actions workflows from creating or approving pull requests. Allowing workflows, or any other automation, to create or approve pull requests could be a security risk if the pull request is merged without proper oversight. By default, when you create a new organization, workflows are not allowed to create or approve pull requests. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/actions/security-guides/security-hardening-for-github-actions#preventing-github-actions-from-creating-or-approving-pull-requests https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/organizations/managing-organization-settings/disabling-or-limiting-github-actions-for-your-organization#preventing-github-actions-from-creating-or-approving-pull-requests "
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Code, planning, and automation' section of the sidebar, click on Actions -> General. \n3. Under the 'Workflow permissions' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Allow GitHub Actions to create and approve pull requests' setting is disabled.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Code, planning, and automation' section of the sidebar, click on Actions -> General. \n3. Under the 'Workflow permissions' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Allow GitHub Actions to create and approve pull requests' setting is disabled.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4320](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4320)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4320 - Organization - Workflow permissions - Prevent GitHub Actions workflows from creating or approving pull requests (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4320"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4330",
              "name": "GhOrganizationActionsPoliciesLimitSpecificActionsAndReusableWorkflowsToRun",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Limit GitHub Actions for your organization. Allow only specific actions and reusable workflows to run. Allow enterprise actions and reusable workflow. When you choose 'Allow enterprise, and select non-enterprise, actions and reusable workflows', local actions and reusable workflows are allowed, and there are additional options for allowing other specific actions and reusable workflows. Allow actions created by GitHub. Allow specified actions and reusable workflows: You can restrict workflows to use actions and reusable workflows in specific organizations and repositories. Specified actions cannot be set to more than 1000.  Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/organizations/managing-organization-settings/disabling-or-limiting-github-actions-for-your-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Code, planning, and automation' section of the sidebar, click on Actions -> General. \n3. Under the 'Policies' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Allow enterprise, and select non-enterprise, actions and reusable workflows' setting is selected. \n5. Ensure 'Allow actions created by GitHub' is checked. \n6. Using the field 'Allow specified actions and reusable workflows', add your required actions and reusable workflows to the list.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Code, planning, and automation' section of the sidebar, click on Actions -> General. \n3. Under the 'Policies' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Allow enterprise, and select non-enterprise, actions and reusable workflows' setting is selected. \n5. Ensure 'Allow actions created by GitHub' is checked. \n6. Using the field 'Allow specified actions and reusable workflows', add your required actions and reusable workflows to the list.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4330](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4330)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4330 - Organization - GitHub Actions - Allow only specific actions and reusable workflows to run (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4330"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4375",
              "name": "GhOrganizationActionsSecretsLimitOrganizationSecretsUsage",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Minimize the usage and sharing of your organization secrets. Protect your secrets by declaring them as environment secrets and use required reviewers to protect environment secrets. The organization secret visibility should be limited to only fewer selected repositories. Anyone with collaborator access to the repositories with access to a secret or variable can use it for Actions. They are not passed to workflows that are triggered by a pull request from a fork. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/actions/security-guides/security-hardening-for-github-actions#using-secrets https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/actions/security-guides/using-secrets-in-github-actions"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Security' section of the sidebar, select Secrets and variables, then click Actions. \n3. Click the 'Secrets' tab. \n4. Review the list and maintain it at a bare minimum. \n5. Ensure the 'Visibility' setting is 'selected repositories'.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Security' section of the sidebar, select Secrets and variables, then click Actions. \n3. Click the 'Secrets' tab. \n4. Review the list and maintain it at a bare minimum. \n5. Ensure the 'Visibility' setting is 'selected repositories'.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4375](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4375)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4375 - Organization - GitHub Actions Secrets - Minimize the usage and sharing of your organization secrets (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4375"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4380",
              "name": "GhOrganizationActionsSecretsRestrictVisibilityToSelectedRepos",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Limit the usage of organization secrets. Protect your secrets by declaring them as environment secrets and use required reviewers to protect environment secrets. The organization secret visibility should be limited to only selected repositories. Anyone with collaborator access to the repositories with access to a secret or variable can use it for Actions. They are not passed to workflows that are triggered by a pull request from a fork. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/actions/security-guides/security-hardening-for-github-actions#using-secrets https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/actions/security-guides/using-secrets-in-github-actions"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Security' section of the sidebar, select Secrets and variables, then click Actions. \n3. Click the 'Secrets' tab. \n4. Find the secret and click on edit pencil icon. \n5. Ensure the 'Repository access' setting is 'Selected repositories'.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Security' section of the sidebar, select Secrets and variables, then click Actions. \n3. Click the 'Secrets' tab. \n4. Find the secret and click on edit pencil icon. \n5. Ensure the 'Repository access' setting is 'Selected repositories'.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4380](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4380)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4380 - Organization - GitHub Actions Secrets - Restrict the access and visibility of an organization secret to only selected repositories (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4380"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4381",
              "name": "GhOrganizationActionsSecretsRestrictAccessToFewerSelectedRepos",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Minimize the sharing of your organization secrets to a smaller set of specific repositories. Limit the usage of organization secrets. Protect your secrets by declaring them as environment secrets and use required reviewers to protect environment secrets. The organization secret visibility should be limited to fewer selected repositories. Anyone with collaborator access to the repositories with access to a secret or variable can use it for Actions. They are not passed to workflows that are triggered by a pull request from a fork. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/actions/security-guides/security-hardening-for-github-actions#using-secrets https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/actions/security-guides/using-secrets-in-github-actions"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Security' section of the sidebar, select Secrets and variables, then click Actions. \n3. Click the 'Secrets' tab. \n4. Find the secret and click on edit pencil icon. \n5. Ensure the 'Repository access' setting is 'Selected repositories' and the number of repositories is under 10.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Security' section of the sidebar, select Secrets and variables, then click Actions. \n3. Click the 'Secrets' tab. \n4. Find the secret and click on edit pencil icon. \n5. Ensure the 'Repository access' setting is 'Selected repositories' and the number of repositories is under 10.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4381](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4381)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4381 - Organization - GitHub Actions Secrets - Restrict the sharing of an organization secret to fewer selected repositories (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4381"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4040",
              "name": "GhOrganizationPoliciesRestrictGithubPagesCreation",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Restrict pages creation in your organization to help prevent sensitive information from being exposed. To protect your organization's data, you can configure permissions for creating pages in your organization. Members should not be able to create public sites, visible to anyone. Members should not be able to create private sites, visible to anyone with permission. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/organizations/managing-organization-settings/managing-the-publication-of-github-pages-sites-for-your-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Access' section of the sidebar, click Member privileges. \n3. Under the 'Pages creation' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Public' and 'Private' settings are disabled.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. In the 'Access' section of the sidebar, click Member privileges. \n3. Under the 'Pages creation' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Public' and 'Private' settings are disabled.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4040](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4040)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4040 - Organization - Restrict GitHub Pages Creation - Disallow members to publish sites (High)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4040"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4220",
              "name": "GhOrganizationCredentialauthShouldHaveLogin",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every SAML SSO credential authorization should be associated with a known organization member's login. An empty Login field indicates an orphaned credential (e.g., the user was removed from the org but the credential authorization was not revoked) - a common GitHub Enterprise Cloud audit finding. Such credentials should be revoked. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/rest/orgs/orgs#list-saml-sso-authorizations-for-an-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings -> Authentication security -> SAML single sign-on. \n2. Review 'Authorized credentials' and revoke any belonging to users no longer in the organization. \n3. Document the revocation in the audit trail.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings -> Authentication security -> SAML single sign-on. \n2. Review 'Authorized credentials' and revoke any belonging to users no longer in the organization. \n3. Document the revocation in the audit trail.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4220](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4220)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4220 - Organization - Organization Credential Authorizations - Should record the authorizing login (High)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4220"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4225",
              "name": "GhOrganizationCredentialauthShouldHaveType",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every SAML SSO credential authorization records the credential kind (one of 'personal access token', 'SSH key', or 'GitHub app'). A missing or blank credential type indicates an unrecognized credential class that bypasses standard auditing - typically a GitHub API change or a partially-migrated credential. Such records require manual review. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/rest/orgs/orgs#list-saml-sso-authorizations-for-an-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings -> Authentication security -> SAML single sign-on -> Authorized credentials. \n2. For credentials with an unknown type, contact the user and confirm they are still in active use. \n3. Revoke unrecognized credential types.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings -> Authentication security -> SAML single sign-on -> Authorized credentials. \n2. For credentials with an unknown type, contact the user and confirm they are still in active use. \n3. Revoke unrecognized credential types.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4225](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4225)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4225 - Organization - Organization Credential Authorizations - Should specify a credential type (High)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4225"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4400",
              "name": "GhOrganizationDependabotSecretVisibilityShouldNotBeAll",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Org-level Dependabot secrets with visibility 'all' are exposed to every repository in the organization, including newly-created ones. This violates least-privilege: Dependabot secrets are typically registry credentials (npm, PyPI, container registries) and should be scoped to the repos that genuinely need them via 'selected' visibility. The 'private' visibility (private repos only) is acceptable when ALL private repos legitimately consume the secret. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/dependabot/working-with-dependabot/configuring-access-to-private-registries-for-dependabot"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Dependabot. \n2. For each secret with visibility 'All repositories', change to 'Private repositories' or 'Selected repositories' and pick only the repos that need access.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Dependabot. \n2. For each secret with visibility 'All repositories', change to 'Private repositories' or 'Selected repositories' and pick only the repos that need access.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4400](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4400)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4400 - Organization - Dependabot Secret - Visibility should not be 'all' (High)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4400"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4420",
              "name": "GhOrganizationCodespacesSecretVisibilityShouldNotBeAll",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Org-level Codespaces secrets with visibility 'all' are exposed in every codespace launched from any repository in the organization. Codespaces secrets are typically API keys or tokens used inside the dev environment; over-broad visibility means any developer launching any repo's codespace inherits credentials they may not need - a violation of least-privilege and a data-residency concern when codespaces span regions. Use 'selected' visibility. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/codespaces/managing-codespaces-for-your-organization/managing-development-environment-secrets-for-your-repository-or-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Codespaces. \n2. For each secret with visibility 'All repositories', change to 'Private repositories' or 'Selected repositories' as appropriate.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Codespaces. \n2. For each secret with visibility 'All repositories', change to 'Private repositories' or 'Selected repositories' as appropriate.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4420](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4420)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4420 - Organization - Codespaces Secret - Visibility should not be 'all' (High)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4420"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4230",
              "name": "GhOrganizationCredentialauthShouldHaveTitle",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every PAT or SSH key authorized for SAML SSO should carry a meaningful title or note describing its intended use (e.g., 'CI build agent prod-east-1', 'developer-laptop-2'). Untitled credentials are difficult to revoke confidently during incident response because their owner and purpose are unknown. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/managing-your-personal-access-tokens"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to your User Settings -> Developer settings -> Personal access tokens. \n2. For each token, edit the 'Note' to describe its purpose. \n3. Re-authorize the token for the SSO-protected organization.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to your User Settings -> Developer settings -> Personal access tokens. \n2. For each token, edit the 'Note' to describe its purpose. \n3. Re-authorize the token for the SSO-protected organization.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4230](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4230)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4230 - Organization - Organization Credential Authorizations - Should have a descriptive title (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4230"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4245",
              "name": "GhOrganizationMemberShouldHaveLogin",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every organization member record should carry a non-empty login. Members without logins are typically a result of stale data, deleted GitHub accounts that were not cleaned up, or in-flight membership changes. Such records should be removed from the organization. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/organizations/managing-membership-in-your-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization -> People. \n2. Identify any rows with missing or anonymized usernames. \n3. Remove them from the organization.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization -> People. \n2. Identify any rows with missing or anonymized usernames. \n3. Remove them from the organization.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4245](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4245)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4245 - Organization - Organization Member - Should have a login name (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4245"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4250",
              "name": "GhOrganizationMemberTypeShouldBeUser",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Organization members should be of type 'User'. Bot accounts that appear as full members (rather than as integrations or apps) typically indicate misconfigured automations - bots should be installed as GitHub Apps with scoped permissions, NOT added as full org members with the broad permission surface that membership grants. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/about-creating-github-apps/about-creating-github-apps"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization -> People. \n2. Identify any 'Bot' type members. \n3. Convert each bot to a GitHub App with the minimum required scopes, then remove the bot from membership.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization -> People. \n2. Identify any 'Bot' type members. \n3. Convert each bot to a GitHub App with the minimum required scopes, then remove the bot from membership.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4250](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4250)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4250 - Organization - Organization Member - Type should be 'User' (not 'Bot') (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4250"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4265",
              "name": "GhOrganizationOutsidecollaboratorShouldHaveLogin",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every outside collaborator record should carry a non-empty login. Records with missing logins typically indicate a stale state where the collaborator was removed from GitHub but not yet purged from the org's collaborator list. Such records should be removed. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/organizations/managing-user-access-to-your-organizations-repositories/managing-outside-collaborators/removing-an-outside-collaborator-from-an-organization-repository"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization -> Outside collaborators. \n2. Identify rows with missing or anonymized usernames. \n3. Remove them from the organization.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization -> Outside collaborators. \n2. Identify rows with missing or anonymized usernames. \n3. Remove them from the organization.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4265](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4265)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4265 - Organization - Outside Collaborator - Should have a login name (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4265"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4410",
              "name": "GhOrganizationActionsVariableVisibilityShouldNotBeAll",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Org-level Actions variables with visibility 'all' are exposed to every repository in the organization, including newly-created ones. Variables (unlike secrets) have their VALUES returned by the GitHub REST API, so leaking them through over-broad visibility may expose configuration data, hostnames, account IDs, or feature flags that adversaries can use during reconnaissance. Use 'selected' visibility to scope variables to only the repos that need them. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/learn-github-actions/variables"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions -> Variables. \n2. For each variable with visibility 'All repositories', change to 'Private repositories' or 'Selected repositories'.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions -> Variables. \n2. For each variable with visibility 'All repositories', change to 'Private repositories' or 'Selected repositories'.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4410](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4410)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4410 - Organization - Actions Variable - Visibility should not be 'all' (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4410"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS4510",
              "name": "GhOrganizationShouldHaveProfile",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Make sure to complete the profile for your organization. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/repositories/creating-and-managing-repositories/creating-a-new-repository"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. Select the 'General' section of the sidebar. \n3. Ensure the profile is complete by filling in the fields: Name, Description, Url, Location and Picture.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Organization Settings. \n2. Select the 'General' section of the sidebar. \n3. Ensure the profile is complete by filling in the fields: Name, Description, Url, Location and Picture.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS4510](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4510)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS4510 - Organization - Organization should always have a complete profile (Low)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS4510"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5010",
              "name": "GhRepositoryPoliciesDisablePrivateForking",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Repository forking should be disabled. Organizations should never allow private or internal repositories to be forked. You can help prevent sensitive information from being exposed by disabling the ability to fork repositories in your organization. By default, new organizations are configured to disallow the forking of private and internal repositories. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/code-security/getting-started/best-practices-for-preventing-data-leaks-in-your-organization#prevent-data-leaks https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/managing-repository-settings/managing-the-forking-policy-for-your-repository"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository Settings. \n2. In the 'General' section of the sidebar, go to 'Features' section. \n3. Under the 'Features' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Allow forking' setting is disabled.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository Settings. \n2. In the 'General' section of the sidebar, go to 'Features' section. \n3. Under the 'Features' section. \n4. Ensure the 'Allow forking' setting is disabled.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5010](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5010)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5010 - Repository - Disable Forking for Private or Internal Repository (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5010"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5050",
              "name": "GhRepositoryVisibilityPrivate",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Repository visibility should always be private or internal. Secure your code by converting public repositories to private whenever appropriate. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/code-security/getting-started/best-practices-for-preventing-data-leaks-in-your-organization#prevent-data-leaks https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/managing-repository-settings/setting-repository-visibility"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository Settings. \n2. In the 'General' section of the sidebar, go to 'Danger Zone' section. \n3. Under the 'Change repository visibility' section. \n4. Ensure the message 'This repository is currently private/internal.' is displayed.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository Settings. \n2. In the 'General' section of the sidebar, go to 'Danger Zone' section. \n3. Under the 'Change repository visibility' section. \n4. Ensure the message 'This repository is currently private/internal.' is displayed.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5050](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5050)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5050 - Repository - Repository visibility should be private (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5050"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5500",
              "name": "GhRepositorySecurityVulnerabilityalertCriticalSeverityIsOpen",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Ensure the critical alert with a dependency affected by a security vulnerability is resolved. Dependabot alerts tell you when your code depends on a package that is insecure. You should upgrade to a secure version of the package as soon as possible. If your code depends on a package with a security vulnerability, this can cause a range of problems for your project or the people who use it. Using a vulnerable package makes you a soft target for malicious users looking to exploit your system. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/dependabot/dependabot-alerts/about-dependabot-alerts https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/code-security/dependabot/dependabot-alerts/viewing-and-updating-dependabot-alerts#reviewing-and-fixing-alerts https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/learning-about-github/about-github-advanced-security"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to your Repository -> Security tab. \n2. In the 'Vulnerability alerts' section of the sidebar, select 'Dependabot' section. \n3. On top of the list, search and select the critical alert. \n4. Review and fix the alert.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to your Repository -> Security tab. \n2. In the 'Vulnerability alerts' section of the sidebar, select 'Dependabot' section. \n3. On top of the list, search and select the critical alert. \n4. Review and fix the alert.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5500](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5500)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5500 - Repository - Security - Vulnerability Alert - Ensure that the Critical severity Dependabot alert is addressed (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5500"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5550",
              "name": "GhRepositorySecurityVulnerabilityalertsNoOpenCriticalSeverity",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Ensure there are no Dependabot alerts for your repository with a dependency affected by a security vulnerability. Dependabot alerts tell you when your code depends on a package that is insecure. You should upgrade to a secure version of the package as soon as possible. If your code depends on a package with a security vulnerability, this can cause a range of problems for your project or the people who use it. Using a vulnerable package makes you a soft target for malicious users looking to exploit your system. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/dependabot/dependabot-alerts/about-dependabot-alerts https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/code-security/dependabot/dependabot-alerts/viewing-and-updating-dependabot-alerts https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/learning-about-github/about-github-advanced-security"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to your Repository -> Security tab. \n2. In the 'Vulnerability alerts' section of the sidebar, select 'Dependabot' section. \n3. On top of the list, click on 'Severity' filter. \n4. Ensure there are no 'Critical' alerts for your repository.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to your Repository -> Security tab. \n2. In the 'Vulnerability alerts' section of the sidebar, select 'Dependabot' section. \n3. On top of the list, click on 'Severity' filter. \n4. Ensure there are no 'Critical' alerts for your repository.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5550](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5550)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5550 - Repository - Security - Vulnerability Alerts - Make sure that there are no unresolved Dependabot alerts of Critical severity (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5550"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5600",
              "name": "GhRepositoryBranchprotectionrulesProtectYourMostImportantBranches",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Protect your most important branches by setting branch protection rules. You can enforce certain workflows or requirements before a collaborator can push changes to a branch in your repository, including merging a pull request into the branch, by creating a branch protection rule. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/about-protected-branches https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/managing-a-branch-protection-rule"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to your Repository Settings. \n2. In the 'Code and automation' section of the sidebar, select 'Branches' section. \n3. Click on 'Add branch protection rule' button. \n4. Follow the steps to protect your most important branches.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to your Repository Settings. \n2. In the 'Code and automation' section of the sidebar, select 'Branches' section. \n3. Click on 'Add branch protection rule' button. \n4. Follow the steps to protect your most important branches.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5600](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5600)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5600 - Repository - Branch Protection Rules - Protect your most important branches (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5600"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5800",
              "name": "GhRepositoryCodespaceShouldNotBeFailed",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A codespace in the 'Failed' state typically indicates an unrecoverable provisioning error: out-of-resource conditions, devcontainer build failures, or a deleted upstream image. Failed codespaces consume billing slots and obscure inventory dashboards. They should be deleted and re-created. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/codespaces/managing-your-codespaces/managing-your-codespaces"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to https://github.com/codespaces or your organization's Codespaces dashboard. \n2. Identify any codespaces in 'Failed' state. \n3. Delete each one and re-create it from the source repository.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to https://github.com/codespaces or your organization's Codespaces dashboard. \n2. Identify any codespaces in 'Failed' state. \n3. Delete each one and re-create it from the source repository.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5800](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5800)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5800 - Repository - Codespace - Should not be in 'Failed' state (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5800"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5850",
              "name": "GhRepositoryCollaboratorOutsideShouldNotHaveAdmin",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When the per-repo collaborator audit (G2.4) lists a collaborator with Affiliation = 'outside' AND Permissions.Admin = true, this is a critical finding: an external user has admin rights on a specific repository. This is the per-repo equivalent of the org-level outside-collaborator audit and surfaces overrides where an outside collaborator was granted admin on a single repo despite org-level policy. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/organizations/managing-user-access-to-your-organizations-repositories/managing-outside-collaborators/adding-outside-collaborators-to-repositories-in-your-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository Settings -> Collaborators and teams. \n2. Identify outside collaborators with admin role. \n3. Downgrade their permission to Maintain, Write, Triage, or Read as appropriate.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository Settings -> Collaborators and teams. \n2. Identify outside collaborators with admin role. \n3. Downgrade their permission to Maintain, Write, Triage, or Read as appropriate.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5850](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5850)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5850 - Repository - Repository Collaborator - Outside collaborators should not have admin permission (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5850"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5525",
              "name": "GhRepositorySecurityVulnerabilityalertHighSeverityIsOpen",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Ensure the critical alert with a dependency affected by a security vulnerability is resolved. Dependabot alerts tell you when your code depends on a package that is insecure. You should upgrade to a secure version of the package as soon as possible. If your code depends on a package with a security vulnerability, this can cause a range of problems for your project or the people who use it. Using a vulnerable package makes you a soft target for malicious users looking to exploit your system. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/dependabot/dependabot-alerts/about-dependabot-alerts https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/code-security/dependabot/dependabot-alerts/viewing-and-updating-dependabot-alerts#reviewing-and-fixing-alerts https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/learning-about-github/about-github-advanced-security"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to your Repository -> Security tab. \n2. In the 'Vulnerability alerts' section of the sidebar, select 'Dependabot' section. \n3. On top of the list, search and select the high alert. \n4. Review and fix the alert.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to your Repository -> Security tab. \n2. In the 'Vulnerability alerts' section of the sidebar, select 'Dependabot' section. \n3. On top of the list, search and select the high alert. \n4. Review and fix the alert.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5525](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5525)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5525 - Repository - Security - Vulnerability Alert - Ensure that the High severity Dependabot alert is addressed (High)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5525"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5575",
              "name": "GhRepositorySecurityVulnerabilityalertsNoOpenHighSeverity",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Ensure there are no Dependabot alerts for your repository with a dependency affected by a security vulnerability. Dependabot alerts tell you when your code depends on a package that is insecure. You should upgrade to a secure version of the package as soon as possible. If your code depends on a package with a security vulnerability, this can cause a range of problems for your project or the people who use it. Using a vulnerable package makes you a soft target for malicious users looking to exploit your system. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/dependabot/dependabot-alerts/about-dependabot-alerts https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/code-security/dependabot/dependabot-alerts/viewing-and-updating-dependabot-alerts https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/learning-about-github/about-github-advanced-security"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to your Repository -> Security tab. \n2. In the 'Vulnerability alerts' section of the sidebar, select 'Dependabot' section. \n3. On top of the list, click on 'Severity' filter. \n4. Ensure there are no 'High' alerts for your repository.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to your Repository -> Security tab. \n2. In the 'Vulnerability alerts' section of the sidebar, select 'Dependabot' section. \n3. On top of the list, click on 'Severity' filter. \n4. Ensure there are no 'High' alerts for your repository.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5575](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5575)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5575 - Repository - Security - Vulnerability Alerts - Make sure that there are no unresolved Dependabot alerts of High severity (High)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5575"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5700",
              "name": "GhRepositoryRulesetsEnforcementShouldBeActive",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Repository or Organization Rulesets are the modern (replacement) protection primitive for branches, tags, and pushes. The 'enforcement' field controls whether a ruleset is enforced ('active'), running in dry-run mode ('evaluate'), or completely turned off ('disabled'). For production-grade compliance, rulesets should always be 'active' so that bypass attempts are blocked rather than just observed. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-rulesets/about-rulesets"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Rules -> Rulesets. \n2. Open each ruleset. \n3. Set 'Enforcement status' to 'Active'.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Rules -> Rulesets. \n2. Open each ruleset. \n3. Set 'Enforcement status' to 'Active'.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5700](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5700)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5700 - Repository - Repository Rulesets - Enforcement should be 'active' (not evaluate or disabled) (High)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5700"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5805",
              "name": "GhRepositoryCodespaceShouldNotBeUnknown",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An 'Unknown' state codespace indicates that GitHub cannot determine the runtime status - typically due to backend connectivity issues or a half-deleted resource. Codespaces in this state should be reviewed and deleted if they cannot be transitioned back to a known state. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/codespaces/troubleshooting/troubleshooting-creation-and-deletion-of-codespaces"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to https://github.com/codespaces. \n2. For each codespace in 'Unknown' state, attempt to start it. \n3. If start fails or stays Unknown, delete the codespace.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to https://github.com/codespaces. \n2. For each codespace in 'Unknown' state, attempt to start it. \n3. If start fails or stays Unknown, delete the codespace.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5805](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5805)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5805 - Repository - Codespace - Should not be in 'Unknown' state (High)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5805"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5855",
              "name": "GhRepositoryCollaboratorOutsideRoleShouldNotBeAdmin",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "GitHub's role-based collaborator model surfaces RoleName ('read' / 'triage' / 'write' / 'maintain' / 'admin' / custom) alongside the per-permission booleans. When an outside collaborator's RoleName is 'admin', they hold the highest role grantable on a repository, regardless of which Permissions.* booleans are flipped. This is a critical finding that complements the Permissions.Admin check - the role and the booleans should both be audited. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/organizations/managing-user-access-to-your-organizations-repositories/repository-roles-for-an-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository Settings -> Collaborators and teams. \n2. Identify outside collaborators with role 'Admin'. \n3. Change their role to 'Maintain' or lower.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository Settings -> Collaborators and teams. \n2. Identify outside collaborators with role 'Admin'. \n3. Change their role to 'Maintain' or lower.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5855](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5855)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5855 - Repository - Repository Collaborator - Outside collaborators should not hold the 'admin' role (High)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5855"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5915",
              "name": "GhRepositoryActionsVariableValueShouldNotContainHttp",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "GitHub Actions variable values are exposed to every workflow run. A value containing 'http://' (instead of 'https://') configures workflows to talk to backend services over plaintext, allowing in-transit interception of the workflow's data and credentials. Update the value to use 'https://'. (HTTPS may not be appropriate for purely internal localhost development URLs that never reach a real network; treat such cases as exemptions.) Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/security-guides/security-hardening-for-github-actions"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions -> Variables. \n2. For each variable whose value contains http://, edit the value to use https:// where the backend supports TLS. \n3. For variables that genuinely need plaintext local URLs, document the exemption.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions -> Variables. \n2. For each variable whose value contains http://, edit the value to use https:// where the backend supports TLS. \n3. For variables that genuinely need plaintext local URLs, document the exemption.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5915](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5915)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5915 - Repository - Actions Variable - Value should not contain insecure 'http://' URLs (High)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5915"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5015",
              "name": "GhRepositoryRepositorySizeLimits",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Repository size limits: We recommend repositories remain small, ideally less than 1 GB, and less than 5 GB is strongly recommended. Smaller repositories are faster to clone and easier to work with and maintain. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/repositories/working-with-files/managing-large-files/about-large-files-on-github#repository-size-limits https://github.com/github/git-sizer"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. You can find advice and a tool for repository analysis in the github/git-sizer repository.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. You can find advice and a tool for repository analysis in the github/git-sizer repository.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5015](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5015)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5015 - Repository - Repository size should remain small: < 1 GB and < 5 GB (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5015"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5020",
              "name": "GhRepositoryDefaultbranchMain",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The repository default branch name should be 'main'. This policy will ensure naming convention is consistent across each repository. You can change the default branch for a particular repository at any time. The default branch is considered the base branch in your repository, against which all pull requests and code commits are automatically made, unless you specify a different branch. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-branches-in-your-repository/changing-the-default-branch"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Navigate to Repository Settings. \n2. In the 'General' section of the sidebar, go to 'Default branch' section. \n3. Ensure 'main' is the default branch. \n4. Navigate to 'Code' and click on the View all branches. \n5. Ensure the 'Default' branch is the 'main' branch.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Navigate to Repository Settings. \n2. In the 'General' section of the sidebar, go to 'Default branch' section. \n3. Ensure 'main' is the default branch. \n4. Navigate to 'Code' and click on the View all branches. \n5. Ensure the 'Default' branch is the 'main' branch.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5020](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5020)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5020 - Repository - Default branch name should be main (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5020"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5060",
              "name": "GhRepositoryPoliciesPullrequestsEnableAllowAutoMerge",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "You can increase development velocity by enabling auto-merge for a pull request so that the pull request will merge automatically when all merge requirements are met. You should allow setting pull requests to merge automatically once all required reviews and status checks have passed. Waits for merge requirements to be met and then merges automatically. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/pull-requests/collaborating-with-pull-requests/incorporating-changes-from-a-pull-request/automatically-merging-a-pull-request https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/configuring-pull-request-merges/managing-auto-merge-for-pull-requests-in-your-repository"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository Settings. \n2. In the 'General' section of the sidebar, go to 'Pull Requests' section. \n3. Ensure the 'Allow auto-merge' setting is enabled.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository Settings. \n2. In the 'General' section of the sidebar, go to 'Pull Requests' section. \n3. Ensure the 'Allow auto-merge' setting is enabled.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5060](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5060)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5060 - Repository - Pull Requests - Enable auto-merge for a pull request (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5060"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5065",
              "name": "GhRepositoryPoliciesPullrequestsEnableAlwaysSuggestUpdatingPrBranches",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Keep your pull request in sync with the base branch. You can give users the ability to always update a pull request branch when it is not up to date with the base branch. Whenever there are new changes available in the base branch, present an “update branch” option in the pull request. Control how and when users are prompted to update their branches if there are new changes available in the base branch. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/configuring-pull-request-merges/managing-suggestions-to-update-pull-request-branches https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/pull-requests/collaborating-with-pull-requests/proposing-changes-to-your-work-with-pull-requests/keeping-your-pull-request-in-sync-with-the-base-branch"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository Settings. \n2. In the 'General' section of the sidebar, go to 'Pull Requests' section. \n3. Ensure the 'Always suggest updating pull request branches' setting is enabled.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository Settings. \n2. In the 'General' section of the sidebar, go to 'Pull Requests' section. \n3. Ensure the 'Always suggest updating pull request branches' setting is enabled.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5065](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5065)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5065 - Repository - Pull Requests - Always suggest updating pull request branches (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5065"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5070",
              "name": "GhRepositoryPoliciesPullrequestsEnableAutomaticallyDeleteHeadBranches",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "You should have head branches automatically deleted after pull requests are merged in your repository. Deleted branches will still be able to be restored. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/configuring-pull-request-merges/managing-the-automatic-deletion-of-branches"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository Settings. \n2. In the 'General' section of the sidebar, go to 'Pull Requests' section. \n3. Ensure the 'Automatically delete head branches' setting is enabled.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository Settings. \n2. In the 'General' section of the sidebar, go to 'Pull Requests' section. \n3. Ensure the 'Automatically delete head branches' setting is enabled.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5070](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5070)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5070 - Repository - Pull Requests - Enable automatically delete head branches after pull requests are merged in your repository (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5070"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5705",
              "name": "GhRepositoryRulesetsShouldNotBeDisabled",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A ruleset with enforcement set to 'disabled' provides no protection at all - it exists only as configuration history. Disabled rulesets typically indicate that a previously enforced policy was deactivated and should be reviewed: either re-enable it ('active'), set it to dry-run ('evaluate') for observation, or remove it entirely. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-rulesets/about-rulesets"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Rules -> Rulesets. \n2. Identify any rulesets in 'Disabled' state. \n3. Decide whether to re-enable ('Active'), observe ('Evaluate'), or delete each disabled ruleset.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Rules -> Rulesets. \n2. Identify any rulesets in 'Disabled' state. \n3. Decide whether to re-enable ('Active'), observe ('Evaluate'), or delete each disabled ruleset.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5705](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5705)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5705 - Repository - Repository Rulesets - Should not be 'disabled' (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5705"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5750",
              "name": "GhRepositoryEnvironmentShouldHaveName",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every deployment environment (e.g., 'production', 'staging', 'qa') should have a non-empty, descriptive name. Environments without names are not addressable from workflows and break the deployment-targeting model. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/deployment/targeting-different-environments/managing-environments-for-deployment"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository Settings -> Environments. \n2. Open each environment. \n3. Ensure the environment name is set and descriptive (e.g., 'production', 'staging').",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository Settings -> Environments. \n2. Open each environment. \n3. Ensure the environment name is set and descriptive (e.g., 'production', 'staging').\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5750](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5750)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5750 - Repository - Repository Environment - Should have a name (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5750"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5810",
              "name": "GhRepositoryCodespaceShouldHaveDisplayName",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Display names make codespaces easy to identify in dashboards and audit logs. Codespaces with empty display names are typically auto-generated and harder to associate with their owner's intent. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/codespaces/customizing-your-codespace/renaming-a-codespace"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to https://github.com/codespaces. \n2. For each codespace, set a meaningful display name (e.g., '<feature-branch>-dev', '<issue-id>-fix').",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to https://github.com/codespaces. \n2. For each codespace, set a meaningful display name (e.g., '<feature-branch>-dev', '<issue-id>-fix').\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5810](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5810)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5810 - Repository - Codespace - Should have a display name (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5810"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5815",
              "name": "GhRepositoryCodespaceShouldHaveLocation",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Each codespace runs in a specific Azure region, surfaced in the 'location' field. A missing location indicates an inventory inconsistency that breaks data-residency compliance reporting and prevents administrators from auditing where development data is being processed. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/codespaces/customizing-your-codespace/setting-your-default-region-for-github-codespaces"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to https://github.com/settings/codespaces. \n2. Verify the codespace default region. \n3. For codespaces missing a location, delete and re-create them so they pick up the correct region assignment.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to https://github.com/settings/codespaces. \n2. Verify the codespace default region. \n3. For codespaces missing a location, delete and re-create them so they pick up the correct region assignment.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5815](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5815)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5815 - Repository - Codespace - Should have a region location (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5815"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5900",
              "name": "GhRepositoryDependabotSecretShouldHaveName",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every Dependabot secret should carry a non-empty name (the GitHub Actions workflow references the secret by name via secrets.<NAME>). Records with empty names are typically mid-creation states or API-quirk artifacts that cannot be consumed by Dependabot's automated dependency updates. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/dependabot/working-with-dependabot/managing-encrypted-secrets-for-dependabot"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Dependabot. \n2. Identify any secrets without names. \n3. Delete or rename them.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Dependabot. \n2. Identify any secrets without names. \n3. Delete or rename them.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5900](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5900)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5900 - Repository - Dependabot Secret - Should have a name (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5900"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5930",
              "name": "GhRepositoryCodespacesSecretShouldHaveName",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every Codespaces secret should carry a non-empty name. The codespace devcontainer references the secret via the environment variable substitution mechanism, so an unnamed secret cannot be consumed and only clutters the secrets list. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/codespaces/managing-codespaces-for-your-organization/managing-development-environment-secrets-for-your-repository-or-organization"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Codespaces. \n2. Delete or rename any secret with an empty name.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Codespaces. \n2. Delete or rename any secret with an empty name.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5930](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5930)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5930 - Repository - Codespaces Secret - Should have a name (Medium)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5930"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5030",
              "name": "GhRepositoryShouldHaveDescription",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Make sure to provide a description for your repository. Adding a description will help users understand its purpose and when to use it. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/repositories/creating-and-managing-repositories/creating-a-new-repository"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to your Repository. \n2. Select the 'Code' section. \n3. Under the 'About' section, click on the settings icon to edit the repository details. \n4. Ensure the 'Description' field is not empty. Provide a short description of this repository.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to your Repository. \n2. Select the 'Code' section. \n3. Under the 'About' section, click on the settings icon to edit the repository details. \n4. Ensure the 'Description' field is not empty. Provide a short description of this repository.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5030](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5030)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5030 - Repository - Repository should always have a description (Low)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5030"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5040",
              "name": "GhRepositoryShouldHaveTopics",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Classifying your repository with topics. To help other people find and contribute to your project, you can add topics to your repository related to your project's intended purpose, subject area, affinity groups, or other important qualities. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/classifying-your-repository-with-topics"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to your Repository. \n2. Select the 'Code' section. \n3. Under the 'About' section, click on the settings icon to edit the repository details. \n4. Ensure the 'Topics' field is not empty.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to your Repository. \n2. Select the 'Code' section. \n3. Under the 'About' section, click on the settings icon to edit the repository details. \n4. Ensure the 'Topics' field is not empty.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5040](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5040)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5040 - Repository - Repository should be classified with topics (Low)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5040"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5710",
              "name": "GhRepositoryRulesetsShouldTargetBranchRefs",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Rulesets target one of three ref kinds: 'branch' (most common for Git protection), 'tag' (for release tag immutability), or 'push' (for org-wide push restrictions). For repositories that enforce branch-based protection, the primary rulesets should target 'branch' refs. Use additional rulesets for tag immutability and push policies. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-rulesets/creating-rulesets-for-a-repository"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository Settings -> Rules -> Rulesets. \n2. For your primary protection ruleset, ensure 'Target' is 'Branch'. \n3. Add separate rulesets for tag and push protection as needed.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository Settings -> Rules -> Rulesets. \n2. For your primary protection ruleset, ensure 'Target' is 'Branch'. \n3. Add separate rulesets for tag and push protection as needed.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5710](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5710)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5710 - Repository - Repository Rulesets - Should target branch refs (Low)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5710"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5715",
              "name": "GhRepositoryRulesetsShouldHaveName",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every ruleset should have a non-empty, descriptive name that conveys its intent (e.g., 'Production branch protection', 'Tag immutability'). Rulesets without names are difficult to audit, hard to reference in incident response, and tend to drift out of step with their original purpose. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-rulesets/creating-rulesets-for-a-repository"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Rules -> Rulesets. \n2. Open each ruleset. \n3. Set the 'Ruleset name' field to something descriptive of its intent.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Rules -> Rulesets. \n2. Open each ruleset. \n3. Set the 'Ruleset name' field to something descriptive of its intent.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5715](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5715)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5715 - Repository - Repository Rulesets - Should have a descriptive name (Low)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5715"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5755",
              "name": "GhRepositoryEnvironmentShouldHaveHtmlUrl",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every deployment environment exposes an HTML URL that documents its configuration page. A missing HtmlUrl typically indicates a partially-created environment or an API quirk that should be reviewed - environments without an HTML URL are difficult to navigate to from incident-response runbooks. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/deployment/targeting-different-environments/managing-environments-for-deployment"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository Settings -> Environments. \n2. Open each environment in the GitHub web UI to confirm the page is reachable. \n3. Re-create or remove environments that fail to render.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository Settings -> Environments. \n2. Open each environment in the GitHub web UI to confirm the page is reachable. \n3. Re-create or remove environments that fail to render.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5755](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5755)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5755 - Repository - Repository Environment - Should have a stable HTML URL (Low)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5755"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5760",
              "name": "GhRepositoryEnvironmentShouldHaveNodeId",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every GitHub environment carries a GraphQL node_id that uniquely identifies it across the platform. A missing or empty NodeId indicates an environment that cannot be cross-referenced via GitHub's GraphQL API - this typically points to a partially-provisioned environment that should be re-created or deleted. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/graphql/guides/using-global-node-ids"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository Settings -> Environments. \n2. For environments that cannot be queried via GraphQL, delete and re-create them via the web UI to obtain a fresh node identifier.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository Settings -> Environments. \n2. For environments that cannot be queried via GraphQL, delete and re-create them via the web UI to obtain a fresh node identifier.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5760](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5760)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5760 - Repository - Repository Environment - Should have a node identifier (Low)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5760"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5765",
              "name": "GhRepositoryEnvironmentShouldHaveUrl",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every GitHub environment exposes a REST API URL that infrastructure-as-code and CI/CD systems use to read or update environment configuration. A missing URL field indicates an environment that cannot be programmatically updated, which breaks GitOps and IaC reconciliation. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/rest/deployments/environments"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Confirm that each environment is reachable via the REST endpoint /repos/{owner}/{repo}/environments/{environment_name}. \n2. Re-create environments missing a REST URL.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Confirm that each environment is reachable via the REST endpoint /repos/{owner}/{repo}/environments/{environment_name}. \n2. Re-create environments missing a REST URL.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5765](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5765)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5765 - Repository - Repository Environment - Should have a REST API URL (Low)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5765"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5920",
              "name": "GhRepositoryActionsVariableShouldHaveValue",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Actions variables with empty values configure workflows to consume a blank string at runtime, which typically causes silent failures (e.g., empty image tag, empty deployment URL). Either set a meaningful value, delete the variable, or replace it with a workflow-level default via env: in the workflow YAML. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/learn-github-actions/variables"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions -> Variables. \n2. For each variable with an empty value, set a value or delete the variable.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions -> Variables. \n2. For each variable with an empty value, set a value or delete the variable.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5920](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5920)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5920 - Repository - Actions Variable - Should have a value (Low)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5920"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS5925",
              "name": "GhRepositoryActionsVariableShouldHaveName",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Every Actions variable must have a non-empty name (the workflow YAML references it as vars.<NAME>). Records with empty names are typically transient API states that cannot be consumed by any workflow and clutter the variables UI. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/learn-github-actions/variables"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions -> Variables. \n2. Identify any variables without names and delete or rename them.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Go to Repository or Organization Settings -> Secrets and variables -> Actions -> Variables. \n2. Identify any variables without names and delete or rename them.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS5925](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5925)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS5925 - Repository - Actions Variable - Should have a name (Low)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS5925"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS6710",
              "name": "GhRepositoryDevsecopsControlSsSecretScanning",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Make sure that Secret Scanning (SS) is in place for your repository. Exposed credentials provide easily exploitable opportunities for attackers. To defend against this threat, secret scanning tools scan for credentials in your source code. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/secret-scanning/about-secret-scanning https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/secret-scanning/configuring-secret-scanning-for-your-repositories https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks  https://appsecmap.com https://owasp.org/www-project-devsecops-guideline/latest/01a-Secrets-Management"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CI Pipeline. \n3. Ensure a secret scanning tool is present in your pipeline. \n4.Or alternatively, you can enable 'Advanced Security' for your repository.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CI Pipeline. \n3. Ensure a secret scanning tool is present in your pipeline. \n4.Or alternatively, you can enable 'Advanced Security' for your repository.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS6710](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6710)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS6710 - AppSec - GH DevSecOps Control - Ensure Secret Scanning (SS) (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6710"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS6720",
              "name": "GhRepositoryDevsecopsControlScaSoftwareCompositionAnalysis",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Make sure that Software Composition Analysis (SCA) / Dependency Scanning is in place for your repository. Scan your application for any vulnerabilities in open source components used in your source code. Dependency scanning detects the open source components used in your source code and detects if there are any associated vulnerabilities. Any found vulnerabilities from open source components get flagged as an alert. Dependency scanning generates an alert for any open-source component, direct or transitive, found to be vulnerable that your code depends upon. Direct vulnerabilities are the libraries your code directly uses. Transitive dependencies are the libraries or other software that direct dependencies use. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/supply-chain-security https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/dependabot https://appsecmap.com https://www.gartner.com/reviews/market/software-composition-analysis-sca"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CI Pipeline. \n3. Ensure a SCA / Dependency scanning tool for dependencies is present in your pipeline.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CI Pipeline. \n3. Ensure a SCA / Dependency scanning tool for dependencies is present in your pipeline.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS6720](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6720)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS6720 - AppSec - GH DevSecOps Control - Ensure Software Composition Analysis (SCA) / Dependency Scanning (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6720"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS6730",
              "name": "GhRepositoryDevsecopsControlSastStaticApplicationSecurityTesting",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Make sure that Static Application Security Testing (SAST) / Code Scanning is in place for your repository. Code scanning lets you analyze the code to find security vulnerabilities and coding errors. SAST tools play a crucial role in identifying security vulnerabilities within source code. These tools analyze code for potential flaws based on predefined security rules and policies. Before their code is compiled, DevSecOps developers begin testing their custom code for security vulnerabilities. This helps them fix issues without affecting the build. SAST tools make this process easier with automatic checks and real-time feedback. Learn more: https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/code-scanning https://appsecmap.com https://owasp.org/www-community/Source_Code_Analysis_Tools https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/business/security-101/what-is-devsecops https://appsecmap.com"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CI Pipeline. \n3. Ensure a SAST / Code scanning tool is present in your pipeline.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CI Pipeline. \n3. Ensure a SAST / Code scanning tool is present in your pipeline.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS6730](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6730)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS6730 - AppSec - GH DevSecOps Control - Ensure Static Application Security Testing (SAST) / Code Scanning (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6730"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS6740",
              "name": "GhRepositoryDevsecopsControlCisContainerImageScanning",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Make sure that Container Image Scanning (CIS) / Image Scanning is in place for your repository. Container image scanning is a crucial aspect of DevSecOps, especially as containers become more prevalent in application deployment. Container scanning is the process and scanning tools used to identify vulnerabilities within containers and their components. It is key to container security, and enables developers and cybersecurity teams to fix security threats in containerized applications before deployment. Container scanning tools, scan containers and compare them against public or proprietary vulnerability databases to uncover potential security issues. Learn more: https://owasp.org/www-project-devsecops-guideline/latest/02f-Container-Vulnerability-Scanning https://www.practical-devsecops.com/top-container-security-tools/ https://www.aquasec.com/cloud-native-academy/container-security/image-scanning/ https://snyk.io/learn/container-security/container-scanning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CI Pipeline. \n3. Ensure a Container Image Scanning (CIS) tool is present in your pipeline.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CI Pipeline. \n3. Ensure a Container Image Scanning (CIS) tool is present in your pipeline.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS6740](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6740)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS6740 - AppSec - GH DevSecOps Control - Ensure Container Image Scanning (CIS) / Container Scanning (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6740"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS6750",
              "name": "GhRepositoryDevsecopsControlIacsIacScanning",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Make sure that Infrastructure as Code (IaC) Scanning (IACS) is in place for your repository. Infrastructure as code (IaC) scanning is the process of analyzing and evaluating the security and compliance of infrastructure code. To reduce the risk that misconfigurations are deployed to the production environment, IaC scanning tools automatically check the infrastructure at the code level for noncompliance with security policies and standards. Learn more: https://appsecmap.com  https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/defender-for-cloud/iac-vulnerabilities https://owasp.org/www-project-devsecops-guideline/latest/ https://www.aquasec.com/cloud-native-academy/devsecops/infrastructure-as-code-iac/ https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/business/security-101/what-is-devsecops https://github.com/aquasecurity/tfsec https://github.com/tenable/terrascan https://github.com/bridgecrewio/checkov"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CI Pipeline. \n3. Ensure a IAC Scanning tool is present in your pipeline.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CI Pipeline. \n3. Ensure a IAC Scanning tool is present in your pipeline.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS6750](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6750)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS6750 - AppSec - GH DevSecOps Control - Ensure Infrastructure as Code Scanning (IACS) (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6750"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS6760",
              "name": "GhRepositoryDevsecopsControlIsInfrastructureScanning",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Make sure that Infrastructure Scanning (IS) is in place for your repository. DevOps does a great job in automating the development and deployment process, but since all moving parts (containers, libraries etc.) are being updated frequently, it is imperative to make sure the infrastructure where you deploy your code is safe. The best way to do that is to incorporate vulnerability scanning into your pipeline. A vulnerability scanner is a computer program designed to assess computers, networks or applications for known weaknesses. These scanners are used to discover the weaknesses of a given system. They are utilized in the identification and detection of vulnerabilities arising from mis-configurations or flawed programming within a network-based asset such as a firewall, router, web server, application server, etc. Learn more: https://owasp.org/www-project-devsecops-guideline/latest/02e-Infrastructure-Vulnerability-Scanning https://www.gartner.com/reviews/market/vulnerability-assessment"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CI/CD Pipeline. \n3. Ensure an Infrastructure Scanning tool is present in your pipeline.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CI/CD Pipeline. \n3. Ensure an Infrastructure Scanning tool is present in your pipeline.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS6760](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6760)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS6760 - AppSec - GH DevSecOps Control - Ensure Infrastructure Scanning (IS) (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6760"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS6770",
              "name": "GhRepositoryDevsecopsControlDastDynamicApplicationSecurityTesting",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Make sure that Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST) is in place for your repository. Dynamic application security testing emulates the methods a bad actor might use to attack an application. This testing occurs while the application is running and is based on predefined use cases. Web Application Vulnerability Scanners are automated tools that scan web applications, normally from the outside, to look for security vulnerabilities such as Cross-site scripting, SQL Injection, Command Injection, Path Traversal and insecure server configuration. DAST is a black-box testing method that scans applications in runtime. DAST is a good method for preventing regressions and does not depend on a specific programming language. Learn more: https://owasp.org/www-community/Vulnerability_Scanning_Tools https://owasp.org/www-community/Free_for_Open_Source_Application_Security_Tools https://appsecmap.com https://snyk.io/learn/application-security/dast-dynamic-application-security-testing/"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CD Pipeline. \n3. Ensure a DAST scanning tool is present in your pipeline.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CD Pipeline. \n3. Ensure a DAST scanning tool is present in your pipeline.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS6770](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6770)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS6770 - AppSec - GH DevSecOps Control - Ensure Dynamic Application Security Testing (DAST) (Critical)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6770"
            },
            {
              "id": "DOS6780",
              "name": "GhRepositoryDevsecopsControlIastInteractiveApplicationSecurityTesting",
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Make sure that Interactive Application Security Testing (IAST) is in place for your repository. During quality assurance testing or when an application is being used, interactive application security tools scan the code to find vulnerabilities and provide reports that identify where in the code the issue is. IAST tools are typically geared to analyze Web Applications and Web APIs, but that is vendor specific. There may be IAST products that can perform good security analysis on non-web applications as well. IAST works best when deployed in a QA environment with automated functional tests running. Learn more: https://owasp.org/www-community/Free_for_Open_Source_Application_Security_Tools https://appsecmap.com https://snyk.io/learn/application-security/iast-interactive-application-security-testing/ https://www.veracode.com/security/interactive-application-security-testing-iast https://owasp.org/www-project-devsecops-guideline/latest/02c-Interactive-Application-Security-Testing"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Recommendation (Remediation Steps):\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CD Pipeline. \n3. Ensure a IAST scanning tool is present in your pipeline.",
                "markdown": "**Recommendation (Remediation Steps):**\n\n1. Navigate to Repository -> Actions. \n2. Open your repository CD Pipeline. \n3. Ensure a IAST scanning tool is present in your pipeline.\n\n---\n\n[View full documentation for DOS6780](https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6780)"
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "DOS6780 - AppSec - GH DevSecOps Control - Ensure Interactive Application Security Testing (IAST) (High)"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://devopsshield.com/security-kb/DOS6780"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
